# Unit tests the one-second budget tolerates, one nextest test id per line.
#
# The budget is 1.000 s of wall clock as observed on the run's own log, held by
# `task rust:test:duration` (scripts/unit_test_duration_gate.py). A test at or
# above it that no line here covers reds the gate.
#
# Numbers below are measured on CI — verify-basic.yml `Smoke (Linux)`,
# ubuntu-latest 4-core — never on a dev box. That runner is roughly 2x slower
# than this project's development machine for the scan-heavy tests and much
# worse for process-spawning ones, and CI is where the gate actually gates. A
# test sitting above ~0.7 s there is a latent red, not a pass; the budget does
# NOT get raised above 1.0 s to buy it headroom. 1.0 s is the contract.
#
# An entry is a DEBT, not a licence. It says "this test is slow and we have not
# fixed it yet", and every line needs a `#` reason above it saying why. The
# list only shrinks. Every line below is a PERMANENT carve-out: something the
# world floors, not something nobody got to yet. There is deliberately no debt
# row left — a temporary entry is marked `# TODO(fast-unit-tests):` so that
# `grep TODO` names the lines owed, and that grep returning nothing is the
# state this file is meant to stay in.
# Never add one to dodge a red you could fix, and never `#[ignore]` a test
# instead — the skip ceiling catches that, this does not.
#
# A line is an `fnmatch` pattern; one carrying no metacharacter is plain
# equality. A pattern is for a FAMILY that is slow for one structural reason —
# it is one line that cannot rot into a dozen stale ones as runners get faster
# and slower. Everything else stays an exact id, so a temporary row can always
# be told from a permanent one by eye.
#
# An UNBOUNDED pattern is refused outright by the reader, not merely frowned
# upon: a line matching `zzz_no_such_crate zzz_no_such_test` — an id belonging
# to no crate — allows every test in the workspace, and one fat-fingered `*`
# would disable this gate while the file still read as a careful list. Pin the
# crate or a name fragment.
#
# A line that matches nothing at or above the budget is not a failure: the
# `live_powershell_*` tests need `pwsh` on PATH and a machine-dependent red is
# worse than a stale line. The gate prints those as
# `stale allowlist candidate: <line>` instead.
#
# Ids are exactly as nextest prints them after the progress counter:
# `<binary id> <test name>`.

# --- Permanent: real external interpreters -------------------------------
# Each of these spawns a real external interpreter (pwsh, nu, elvish, fish);
# the cost is process start, not test logic, and no amount of test work removes
# it. `.config/nextest.toml` already names this family in the `live-shell`
# test-group and serialises it, because concurrent .NET process starts corrupt
# each other's assembly loading. Anchored on the `live_` naming convention, so
# an ordinary ocx_shell unit test that goes slow is still caught — deliberately,
# and it hides nothing today: of the 316 non-`live_` ocx_shell tests the slowest
# is 0.271 s on this dev box and 0.020 s on CI.
#
# The pattern also spans two module paths (`shell::tests::live_*` and
# `shell::hook::live_shell_tests::*`), which is the second reason it is not an
# enumeration: the CI-only over-budget member (1.607 s, 0.978 s here) lives in
# the one a list written off a local run would never have reached.
#
# On CI this covers 11 tests over budget and two more just under it that a
# slower runner will push over — 30.2 s down to 1.8 s over, 0.67 s and 0.62 s
# under, measured on verify-basic.yml run 35521310964.
ocx_shell *live_*

# --- Permanent: a wait the HTTP grammar floors ----------------------------
# RFC 9110 spells `Retry-After` delay-seconds as `1*DIGIT`, and
# `parse_retry_after` (crates/ocx_oci/src/transport_policy.rs) reads it with
# `value.parse::<u64>()`, so one second is the smallest interval a real header
# can state. Getting under the budget would mean either inventing a fractional
# spelling in the production parser for a test's benefit, or deleting the
# `RetryDelay::After(stated)` half of the proof — the only thing establishing
# that the delay is WAITED rather than merely parsed. The clamp sibling at
# 0.004 s already covers "the header is read and routed"; this covers the wait.
ocx_index ocx_index::transport_wire_tests::a_retry_after_on_the_wire_is_waited_out

# --- Permanent: one whole-tree parse per test process ---------------------
# These guards each read every `crates/*/src/**` file and parse it with `syn`
# — 605 files, ~2.4M tokens. The corpus is already parsed once per test rather
# than the three-to-ten times it was (`perf(test): the workspace structure
# guards read the tree once, not ten times`), and that one parse is the floor:
# nextest runs EACH TEST IN ITS OWN PROCESS, so no cache can amortise the walk
# across the guards no matter where it lives. A global syntax-tree cache was
# tried and measured HARMFUL — `no_classification_in_libraries` went 0.92 s to
# 1.82 s on allocator pressure alone from holding 600 trees.
#
# Numbers below are a `taskset -c 0-3` proxy of the CI runner, which is the
# shape that decides this — part of the win is intra-test parallelism, so a
# 32-core figure doubled would understate a 4-core runner rather than bound it.
# Uncontended on a 32-core box every one of them is under 0.7 s.
#
# Do NOT merge these guards together to share a parse: that trades failure
# granularity and the test count, and the count is floored by NEXTEST_FLOOR.
ocx_test_support::workspace_structure every_utility_error_reaching_the_cli_is_armed
ocx_test_support::workspace_structure no_module_path_in_output
ocx_test_support::workspace_structure no_classification_in_libraries
ocx_test_support::workspace_structure no_log_shim
ocx_test_support::workspace_structure every_source_file_is_reachable
ocx_test_support::workspace_structure ssrf_guard_ratchet
ocx_test_support::workspace_structure tests_hold_no_inert_loop

# The last two are here for a DIFFERENT reason and would not be helped by any
# further parse sharing: both are bound by a cargo SUBPROCESS, not by the
# corpus. Their wall-to-CPU ratio is about 1.1x locally and 7.5x on CI, and
# that gap is time spent BLOCKED — cold registry reads, `Cargo.lock` reads and
# waiting on the cargo package-cache lock — which a local box serves from page
# cache, so no local instrument sees it at any load. It is NOT a child process
# an in-process measurement misses: `/usr/bin/time` reports `wait4` rusage and
# does count reaped descendants, which a control disproved. Judge these two by
# wall clock on the runner; a profiler here is measuring the wrong machine. `release_feature_set_excludes_testing_seams` shells out to
# `cargo tree -p ocx --edges normal,build --locked`, a full feature
# resolution. `deps_direction` is the only guard that reads `resolve.nodes`,
# so it needs the full 3.4 MB `cargo metadata` where its siblings moved to
# `--no-deps` (129 KB, and 2.23 s -> 0.09 s for doing so) — which left it the
# sole full-metadata consumer in the binary, paying cold what it used to share
# warm with two others. That is an accepted trade, not a regression to chase.
#
# Both lines below are measured on the runner, across two verify-basic.yml
# runs — 35521310964 and 35524469736, whose runners differed by about 1.2x:
#
#   release_feature_set_excludes_testing_seams   0.898 s   0.958 s
#   deps_direction                               2.499 s   4.076 s
#
# The `taskset -c 0-3` proxy read both as ~0.9 s, so it under-reads by 1.0x on
# one and 2.7-4.5x on the other. A proxy bounds nothing; the runner is the
# authority here.
#
# `release_feature_set_excludes_testing_seams` is UNDER the budget on both, so
# the gate prints it every run as `stale allowlist candidate`. That line is
# expected and the entry stays. Two samples are why: 90% of the budget on one
# runner and 96% on a slightly slower one is not headroom, it is a red waiting
# for a third runner — and it would land on a test nobody touched.
ocx_test_support::workspace_structure release_feature_set_excludes_testing_seams
ocx_test_support::workspace_structure deps_direction
